// ============================================================================
== 1.1 Why the Order Is the Method

Every comprehension paper is built the same way: a passage of three to five paragraphs, then four or five questions, of which at least one asks for the main idea and the rest ask for something the passage states. The candidate who reads the passage first reads four hundred words to answer four questions. The candidate who reads the questions first reads the same four hundred words knowing which four sentences matter — and usually reads no more than those.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_read_cycle.svg",
  "The four moves of a passage question. None of them asks you to remember the passage; all of them ask you to find a sentence.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The order, stated once")[
  + Read the questions *and their options* before the passage.
  + Underline the key word in each question — a name, a phrase in quotation marks, a line number, a NOT.
  + Read the passage once, marking the paragraph that answers each question with its number in the margin.
  + Answer from the marked sentence, reading it twice: once for sense, once for the exact word the option uses.
  + Check by putting the option back into the sentence. If the sentence now says something different, the option is wrong.
]

The preview takes forty seconds and it is the only part of the method that feels like cheating. It is not: the questions are printed before the passage on the screen, and the examiner knows that most candidates ignore them.

#concept-box("What the four questions tell you")[
  A set of questions is a map of the passage. If question 2 quotes *line 12*, the answer is in the second half. If question 4 says *the writer's attitude towards the council*, the passage is about a council and contains an opinion of it. If three questions are about a single paragraph, that paragraph is the centre of the passage — and the main-idea question is almost always answered by the paragraph the other questions cluster around. Read the questions, and you have read the passage's skeleton before you have read a word of it.
]

// ============================================================================
== 1.2 What a Paragraph Is Doing

A passage is not a wall of sentences. It is three or four paragraphs, and each paragraph does one job: it makes a claim, or it supports a claim, or it turns the argument. Once you can see the job, the main idea is the claims joined together and the detail is everything else.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_paragraph.svg",
  "The four parts of a body paragraph. The topic sentence is the part a summary keeps and the part a main-idea question quotes.", width: 100%)

#shortcut-box("Find the topic sentence in five seconds")[
  + Read the *first* sentence of the paragraph. If it makes a claim, it is the topic sentence — and most do.
  + If the first sentence is a question, a quotation, a date or a name, read the *second*: the claim is almost always there.
  + If the paragraph opens with an example (*For instance, in Kano…*), the claim is the sentence *after* the example.
  + If none of the first three sentences makes a claim, read the *last* sentence: some writers build to the point instead of from it.
  + The topic sentence never contains *for example*, *such as*, a percentage or a date. Those words announce support.
]

The four common paragraph shapes are worth naming, because the shape tells you where the claim sits: claim then support (the claim is first), problem then solution (the claim is the solution, at the end), comparison then preference (the claim is the preference), and narrative then lesson (the claim is the last sentence). The paper uses the first shape about half the time and the other three between them the rest.

#trap-box("The paragraph that begins with a fact")[
  A paragraph that opens *Lagos has a population of more than fifteen million* has not yet made a claim — it has given a figure. The claim is the next sentence: *and that figure explains why its transport system is permanently at breaking point*. The examiners use this opening because a figure looks like a topic sentence to a candidate in a hurry. Read one sentence further.
]

// ============================================================================
== 1.3 The Main Idea, the Title and the Purpose

Three questions sound alike and want three different answers. The *main idea* is the claim the passage supports, stated in a sentence. The *title* is the same claim compressed to a phrase. The *purpose* is what the writer was doing when he made the claim — warning, persuading, correcting, describing.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_main_vs_detail.svg",
  "Main-idea questions and detail questions are answered from different places, and an option that answers the wrong one is the commonest wrong answer on the paper.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("The three questions, and how to tell them apart")[
  + *Main idea:* “What is the passage mainly about?”, “Which of these best summarises the passage?” — answer with a claim, in one sentence, covering every paragraph.
  + *Title:* “Which of these would be the best title?” — answer with a phrase, short enough for a title and wide enough to cover the passage.
  + *Purpose:* “The writer's purpose is to…” — answer with a verb: *to warn against*, *to argue that*, *to describe how*, *to correct the belief that*.
]

#concept-box("The two tests a main idea must pass")[
  *The coverage test.* The answer must be supported by every paragraph, not by one. If a paragraph has nothing to do with the option, the option is too narrow.

  *The claim test.* The answer must be something the writer asserts, not something the passage merely mentions. *The passage mentions market traders* is a topic; *the passage argues that market traders keep the city fed while the council ignores them* is a main idea.

  An option that fails the coverage test is too narrow — it is a detail. An option that passes coverage but not the claim test is too broad — it is a topic, not an argument. Both are on the paper, usually one on each side of the right answer.
]

// ============================================================================
== 1.4 The Four Passes

The method is a timetable. Six minutes for a passage and four questions is not much, and it is enough only if the minutes are spent in the right order.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_three_passes.svg",
  "The four passes, the time each takes, and what each one produces. Add them up: five and a half minutes for a passage and four questions.", width: 100%)

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The main idea, and the detail that looks like it",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[Every market day, the road to Kurmi begins to fill before five in the morning. By six, the lorries have taken both lanes, and the okada riders thread between them with loads that double their width. The council has promised a lorry park for eleven years; the file has moved between three departments and been lost once. Meanwhile the traders have built their own order: a stretch of roadside, marked out with stones, that works better than any park the council has drawn. Last year the council tried to clear it. The traders moved two hundred metres and set the stones down again.]
    ]

    #v(3pt)
    Which of the following best summarises the passage?

    A. The council has promised a lorry park for eleven years. \
    B. Traders on the Kurmi road have built their own working order where the council has failed. \
    C. Okada riders carry loads that double their width. \
    D. The council cleared the roadside market last year.],
  [
    *Step 1 — the question type.* It asks for a summary of the whole passage, so every paragraph must support the answer: this is a main-idea question, not a detail question.

    *Step 2 — the claim.* Paragraph one describes the congestion, paragraph two the council's failure, paragraph three the traders' stones. The claim that covers all three is that the traders have produced an order the council has not.

    *Step 3 — test the options against the coverage rule.*

    *A* is true — it is the first clause of paragraph two — but a passage about eleven years of a lost file would not describe the lorries and the stones. It covers one paragraph, so it is too narrow.

    *C* is a detail from paragraph one and covers nothing else, so it fails coverage twice over.

    *D* is a detail from the last two sentences, and it is the opposite of the passage's point: the council cleared the road and the traders simply moved.

    *B* is the only option supported by all three paragraphs, and it makes a claim rather than listing a fact.

    *Check.* Ask which option you could put at the top of the passage as a heading without deleting anything. Only *B* survives: the lorries, the file and the stones are all evidence for it. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The pronoun and its referent",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[The ministry has published new rules for private schools, and it has given them only six weeks to comply. The rules require every school to register its water source and to submit its buildings for inspection. Critics say the six weeks are impossible; the ministry says the deadline is firm.]

    #v(3pt)
    The word *them* in the first sentence refers to
    ]

    A. the new rules \
    B. private schools \
    C. the ministry \
    D. six weeks],
  [
    *Step 1 — find the pronoun.* *Them* is the object of *given*, in the clause *it has given them only six weeks*.

    *Step 2 — substitute each candidate.* “The ministry has published new rules for private schools, and it has given *the new rules* only six weeks to comply” — rules cannot comply, and the clause would say the ministry gave the rules a deadline rather than giving the schools one. “It has given *private schools* only six weeks to comply” makes sense: schools comply, and they are the last plural noun.

    *Step 3 — use the number.* *Them* is plural, which rules out *the ministry* and *six weeks* as a single quantity, and *the new rules* is grammatically possible but semantically wrong.

    *Check.* Read the sentence with the substitute and ask who has to act: the schools register and submit, so they are the ones given six weeks. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "The word the dictionary gets wrong",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[The committee has refused to sanction the new levy until the state government explains how the money will be spent. Its chairman said the members were not opposed to the levy in principle, but that a tax without an account is a levy without a purpose.]

    #v(3pt)
    As used in the passage, *sanction* means
    ]

    A. approve \
    B. penalise \
    C. announce \
    D. collect],
  [
    *Step 1 — the word's two lives.* *Sanction* means *approve* when it is a verb of permission, and *penalise* when it is a noun or a verb of punishment. A candidate who knows only one meaning answers from memory, and the passage is built to punish that.

    *Step 2 — the syntax.* Here *sanction* follows *refused to*, so it is a verb, and the object is *the new levy*: the committee has refused to *do something* to the levy until it is explained.

    *Step 3 — the sense.* The chairman says the members are *not opposed to the levy in principle*, which means they would approve it if the money were accounted for. So the refusal is a refusal to approve, not a refusal to punish. *Penalise* would make the sentence say the committee punished a levy it does not oppose.

    *Check.* Replace the word with each option and read the sentence: “refused to approve the levy until…” is coherent; “refused to penalise the levy” is not. The answer is *A*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "The purpose behind the description",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[We are told that the new bridge will shorten the journey by forty minutes. Nobody is told that the toll will cost a trader half a day's profit, or that the pedestrian walkway has been left out of the design. A bridge that only cars can use is not a bridge for a market town; it is a lane with a toll gate on it.]

    #v(3pt)
    The writer's purpose in the passage is to
    ]

    A. describe the design of a new bridge \
    B. argue that the bridge serves cars at the expense of the town \
    C. explain how a toll gate is built \
    D. compare two market towns],
  [
    *Step 1 — find the verdict.* The last sentence is where the writer stops describing and starts judging: *it is a lane with a toll gate on it*. Purpose questions are answered at the point where the passage stops reporting.

    *Step 2 — name the verb.* The writer is not describing or explaining — the bridge is described only to be criticised. The verb is *argue*.

    *Step 3 — test the options against the verdict.*

    *A* describes the first sentence only, and a passage that merely described a bridge would not end with *not a bridge for a market town*.

    *C* is a detail about a toll gate that the passage never explains.

    *D* mentions market towns, but no second town appears and there is no comparison.

    *B* matches the verdict exactly: cars are served, the trader and the pedestrian are not.

    *Check.* Cover the last sentence. The purpose is no longer clear — which is the proof that the last sentence carries it. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "The claim that arrives last",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[For three years the clinic kept its records in four exercise books, one for each nurse, and no two books agreed. A child vaccinated in January could be vaccinated again in March. When the district office asked how many children had been reached, the answer took a week to produce and was wrong. The exercise books were not the problem. The clinic had never been given a way of keeping one record, and nobody had noticed that four records are worse than none.]
    ]

    #v(3pt)
    Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?

    A. The clinic kept its records in four exercise books. \
    B. Vaccinating a child twice in one year is dangerous. \
    C. The clinic's problem was a missing system, not careless nurses. \
    D. The district office asked for figures it did not need.],
  [
    *Step 1 — locate the claim.* The first three sentences are evidence; the fourth turns the argument (*not the problem*), and the fifth states the claim. This is the build-to-the-point shape, so the topic sentence is last.

    *Step 2 — state the claim in your own words.* The books were fine; what was missing was a single agreed method, and nobody had seen that four records are worse than one.

    *Step 3 — test the options.*

    *A* is the first sentence and nothing more — a true detail with no claim in it.

    *B* is a reasonable invention: the passage says a child could be vaccinated twice, and never says it is dangerous. This is the trap for a candidate who reasons ahead of the text.

    *D* contradicts the passage, which says the district office's question took a week to answer, not that it was unnecessary.

    *C* is the claim: the problem was the missing system, and the nurses are explicitly cleared by *not the problem*.

    *Check.* Put each option at the top of the passage and see whether the exercise books, the double vaccination and the district office all become evidence for it. Only *C* absorbs all three. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/recap_ch01.svg",
  "Chapter 1 recap card: reading for the main idea.", width: 100%)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 1 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    Read the questions first, underline the key word in each, and let them tell you where to read. A paragraph is a claim and its support: the claim is the first sentence, or the sentence after the figure or the example, or the last sentence if the writer builds to it. The main idea is the claim every paragraph supports, stated in one sentence; a detail that appears in the passage is true and is not the main idea, and an option too broad to argue anything is a topic rather than an answer. Work in four passes — preview in forty seconds, skim the topic sentences in ninety, scan the marked sentence in three minutes, and check each choice by putting it back into the sentence it came from. Above all, answer from the sentence and not from memory: the passage holds four hundred words and the answer holds twenty, and the twenty are always in front of you.
  ]
]

== 1.5 Chapter 1 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [“The road to the market fills before dawn, and the council's lorry park has been promised for eleven years.” The main idea of this sentence is that], [the market road is busy before dawn], [a promised lorry park has not been built], [the council's promise and the road's congestion are connected], [the council meets before dawn])

#mcq(2, [“The committee has refused to sanction the levy until the accounts are published.” As used here, *sanction* means], [penalise], [approve], [announce], [collect])

#mcq(3, [“The ministry published the rules and gave the schools six weeks to comply.” The word *the schools* could be replaced by which pronoun without changing the sense?], [it], [them], [they], [their])

#mcq(4, [A passage describes a clinic's records, says the books were not the problem, and ends by saying nobody had noticed that four records are worse than none. The writer's purpose is to], [describe the clinic's exercise books], [argue that the clinic lacked a system rather than diligence], [explain how to keep records], [compare four nurses])

#answer-key-grid(("C", "B", "B", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("C", [the sentence does not merely report two facts; it puts them side by side so that the promise is seen to have failed the congestion. A and B are the two facts on their own, and either one is a detail. D invents a meeting time that the sentence never mentions.]),
  ("B", [*refused to sanction the levy* means refused to approve it, because the next clause says the members are not opposed in principle. A is the other life of the word — penalise — and it is the trap the passage is built on. C and D would not be followed by *until the accounts are published*.]),
  ("B", [the schools are the object of *gave*, and *them* is the object form. A is singular, C is the subject form, and D is possessive; after a verb that takes an object, only *them* will do.]),
  ("B", [the passage corrects an assumption — the books were not the problem — and that correction is the purpose. A is the detail the passage uses, C is a method the passage never gives, and D invents a comparison.]),
))

